          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I stopped my blood pressure tablets two months ago and my home readings are now around 155/95. Is that an emergency, and what does guidance describe about treatment and follow-up?",
          "answer": "If symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, please seek medical advice (diagnostic specialist).\n\nYour blood pressure readings of 155/95 mmHg, after stopping medication two months ago, are not categorized as an emergency by the red-flag table. However, this does not mean the situation is not serious (diagnostic specialist). Your home blood pressure readings of 155/95 mmHg are considered \"Stage 2 hypertension\" according to United States society guidance (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets). This means your systolic blood pressure is at least 140 mmHg or your diastolic blood pressure is at least 90 mmHg (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets). Hypertension is defined as persistently raised pressure in the arteries, sustained over time, not from a single reading; it typically has no symptoms and is often found during routine measurements (diagnostic specialist). Blood pressure naturally varies throughout the day (diagnostic specialist). The fact that your blood pressure rose after stopping medication suggests that the medication was effective in controlling your blood pressure, and your current elevated readings warrant re-evaluation by your doctor (research specialist).\n\nFor most people, the target blood pressure is below 130/80 mmHg (guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention). For Stage 2 hypertension, guidance recommends drug treatment alongside lifestyle measures (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment). Often, treatment begins with two medications because a single agent may not be enough to reach the target (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment). Common first-line drug classes include thiazide-type diuretics, angiotensin-converting-enzyme (ACE) inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs), and dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment).\n\nGuidance on follow-up intervals for hypertension states that after treatment starts or changes, reassessment at roughly monthly intervals is needed until the target blood pressure is reached (diagnostic specialist, guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets). Once readings are stable, a review every three to six months is appropriate (diagnostic specialist, guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
